  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="date">EFFECTIVE 2026-07-18</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <strong>Multiplex collects no data.</strong> There is no account, no analytics, no tracking, no ads, no third-party SDK, and no server of ours. Your bytes travel between your devices and the hosts you add, and nowhere else.
    </div>

    <h2>What the app stores, and where</h2>
    <p>Everything Multiplex needs lives on your device:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Host configurations</strong> (names, addresses, usernames, agent command setups) in the app's local storage.</li>
      <li><strong>Secrets</strong> (passwords and SSH private keys) only in the device <strong>Keychain</strong>, never in plain files.</li>
      <li><strong>Convenience caches</strong>: terminal theme choices, last-known session snapshots so the wall paints instantly, and the free tier's daily agent-command counter.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Deleting the app deletes its local data; Keychain items are removed when you remove the hosts they belong to.</p>

    <h2>Sync between your devices</h2>
    <p>Host records, secrets, and command setups sync through <strong>iCloud Keychain</strong>, Apple's end-to-end encrypted keychain sync. It moves data between your own devices signed into your Apple Account. We operate no sync service and cannot read what syncs; nothing about your hosts ever reaches us.</p>

    <h2>Where your bytes go</h2>
    <p>Multiplex opens network connections only to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The hosts you add</strong>, over SSH (and mosh over UDP when you enable it). Terminal input and output, session probes, and files you attach or drop go directly from your device to that host.</li>
      <li><strong>Apple services the system provides</strong>: iCloud Keychain for sync and the App Store for purchases.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Files, Photos, and Camera</h2>
    <p>Attachments use the system pickers, which run outside the app: Multiplex receives only the items you pick, never your library. A photo taken with the camera (iPad and iPhone) is uploaded straight to your host and is not added to your photo library. Uploads go over the same SSH connection as the terminal.</p>

    <h2>Widgets and Shortcuts</h2>
    <p>Home Screen widgets read a snapshot the app writes into a private App Group container on your device: host names, session names, last-seen screen lines, and timestamps. The snapshot contains <strong>no secrets</strong>, and widgets never open network connections. Shortcuts and the <code>multiplex://</code> scheme open the app itself.</p>

    <h2>Notifications</h2>
    <p>Agent alerts (a Pro feature you enable) are <strong>local notifications</strong> generated on your device from sessions the app is already watching. There is no push service and no notification data leaves the device.</p>

    <h2>Purchases</h2>
    <p>Multiplex is a free download; <strong>Multiplex Pro</strong> is a one-time in-app purchase handled entirely by Apple through the App Store. Apple processes payment and shares no payment details with us. Entitlement checks happen on-device through StoreKit.</p>

    <h2>Permissions the app may request</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>When, and why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Camera</td><td>Only if you choose FILE, then Camera, to shoot a photo straight to your host.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local Network</td><td>Only when a host you added lives on your local network.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Only if you turn on agent alerts.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Photos and Files need no permission grant because the system pickers hand the app only what you select.</p>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, the new version is posted here with a new effective date. Questions are welcome through the support contact on Multiplex's <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6790074057">App Store page</a>.</p>
  </div>
